content.strength_and_weaknesses:
Originality: The idea of the paper is quite novel, but it seems to be not very useful for out-of-domain generalization in which the key challenges are the difference between source domains and the lack of access to the data in the target domain. 

Quality: The technical contributions of the paper are relatively weak. In particular, the authors claim that the main goal of the paper is to address the simplicity bias in domain generalization, but there was not any (mathematical) definition of "simplicity bias", as well as the effect of that in domain generalization. More importantly, since the SDT training is based on the evaluation of the validation loss for each source domain, it is unclear to me why SDT can model help explore more relevant invariant features across domains (mentioned as a conjecture in the paper, line 150-151) - see several existing domain-invariant methods (e.g., [1,2]) for more details. . Also, it seems that the theoretical results in Theorem 1 are developed for the case of linear classifiers (e.g., SVM) only, and it is, therefore, hard to be extended for modern models (e.g., deep learning). 

Clarity: The paper is quite well-written and easy to follow. 

